<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold I, Prince of Anhalt-Dessau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1676">1676</a>–<a href="/years/1747">1747</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Old Dessauer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold I, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, known as the Old Dessauer, ruled the principality from 1693 to 1747 and served as a Prussian field marshal. He drilled the Prussian infantry, introducing the iron ramrod and cadenced marching. He fought in the War of the Spanish Succession and won his last victory at Kesselsdorf in 1745.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-i-prince-of-anhalt-dessau.jpg" alt="Leopold I, Prince of Anhalt-Dessau" width="200" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Lisiewski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_I,_Prince_of_Anhalt-Dessau.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, the ninth of ten children of John George II, Prince of Anhalt-Dessau, and Henriette Catherine, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-henry-prince-of-orange">Frederick Henry, Prince of Orange</a>. He devoted himself to military pursuits from his earliest youth. In 1693 he became colonel of a Prussian regiment and inherited his principality, and for the rest of his life he performed the duties of a sovereign prince and of a Prussian officer together. His first campaign was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1695, where he was present at the Siege of Namur, while his mother managed the principality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His career in important commands began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> in 1701. He had improvements made in the Prussian army, notably the iron ramrod about 1700, and served at the sieges of Kaiserswerth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> in 1702. He fought at Blenheim in 1704, at Cassano in 1705, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1706, where he was the first to enter the enemy entrenchments. In 1712, at the request of the Prussian crown prince Frederick William, he was made a field marshal. In 1715 he commanded an army of 40,000 men in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and defeated the smaller force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> on Rügen.</p><p>In peacetime he trained the Prussian infantry. He was one of the sternest disciplinarians of an age of stern discipline, and his training made the infantry a powerful force. He neglected the cavalry, with results at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mollwitz">Battle of Mollwitz</a> in 1741. He also intervened for Crown Prince Frederick, who had been court-martialled for desertion, and secured his reinstatement in the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, who found him somewhat difficult to manage, Leopold spent most campaigning years up to 1745 commanding an army of observation on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> frontier. On 14 December 1745 he decided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-silesian-war">Second Silesian War</a> with a victory over the Saxons at Kesselsdorf. A devout Lutheran, he prayed before the battle, and Frederick embraced him afterwards. He then retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> and died on 7 April 1747.</p><p>In 1698 he married Anna Louise Föhse, an apothecary's daughter; the marriage was morganatic until an imperial decree of 29 December 1701, and the couple had ten children. His son Leopold II Maximilian succeeded him, and another son, Moritz of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, became the best known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold I of Anhalt-Dessau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Leopold I</b>, the Old Dessauer, was a Prussian field marshal who introduced the iron ramrod and cadenced marching.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1722" aria-label="Dessau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4826 371 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold I, Prince of Anhalt-Dessau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1722">What was here in 1722? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
